WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.506
Cleanup must be correct on every path, not only the happy path.

2
00:00:04.506 --> 00:00:09.763
Early returns, exceptions, validation failures, partial construction, and ownership transfer all complicate manual cleanup.

3
00:00:09.763 --> 00:00:15.020
A design that depends on remembering a cleanup call in every branch is fragile.

4
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.151
Allocation and deallocation mechanisms must match at a defensive engineering level.

5
00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:25.910
Memory and resources should be released using the corresponding mechanism expected by the allocator, library, or resource owner.

6
00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:29.665
Mixing mechanisms can violate lifecycle contracts and make review difficult.

7
00:00:29.665 --> 00:00:32.294
Avoid double cleanup and stale ownership assumptions.

8
00:00:32.294 --> 00:00:40.180
Flags that claim ownership, comments that describe transfer, and raw pointers that sometimes own and sometimes borrow are all warning signs.

9
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.310
The type system should carry as much ownership meaning as practical.

10
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.314
Destructors and RAII wrappers should enforce cleanup discipline.

11
00:00:47.314 --> 00:00:51.820
If a wrapper owns a resource, its destructor should release it once.

12
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.073
Move behavior should transfer ownership clearly.

13
00:00:54.073 --> 00:00:58.580
Copy behavior should be disabled or defined intentionally based on the resource.

14
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:02.710
RAII reduces fragile manual cleanup logic because cleanup follows object lifetime.

15
00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:10.971
Error paths become easier to reason about when local owners release resources automatically and class members clean up in reverse construction order.

16
00:01:10.971 --> 00:01:14.727
Reviewers can focus on ownership boundaries rather than every branch.

17
00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:17.355
Cleanup review should include ownership transfer points.

18
00:01:17.355 --> 00:01:22.988
A function that hands a resource to another owner should not also release it later.

19
00:01:22.988 --> 00:01:27.869
A function that borrows a resource should not silently become responsible for cleanup.

20
00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:32.000
Clear transfer points prevent error paths from relying on stale assumptions.
